course: arts
lesson: 5
title: The songs on everyone's phone: streaming, playlists, earworms
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Streaming means playing music over the internet without owning a copy; the app pays a tiny amount for each play, shared between the record label, the songwriters and the artist, so one play earns the singer a fraction of a penny, not a pound.
explain: The app's "made for you" playlist is not mind reading: an algorithm, a set of rules a computer follows, looks at what you play, skip and like and compares it with millions of other listeners.
explain: Picture a chorus you have hummed for three days: that is an earworm, and almost everyone gets them; it is harmless and fades.
explain: Nothing is truly free: free apps are paid for by adverts, pirate sites pay musicians nothing and can hide viruses, and blasting headphones can damage your hearing for good.
explain: Taste is personal: Gen Z might call a song "bussin'" (really good) or say it "ate and left no crumbs" (did it perfectly), a sister may call it "mid" (average), and a Ukrainian dad, "tato", may say "back in my day" about his old rock band; nobody is wrong about liking things.
fact: A song stuck in your head is called an earworm; scientists call it involuntary musical imagery, and most people get them.
fact: One study reported that chewing gum may help a little with an earworm, and listening to the whole song to the end helps some people.
fact: Streaming services such as Spotify are reported to pay roughly a fraction of a penny per play, split between the label, the songwriters and the artist; going to shows or buying music straight from the artist is reported to help them far more.
fact: A royalty is money paid to music makers when their work is used.
fact: Music apps use recommendation algorithms built from your own listening history and other people's.
fact: Free music apps earn money from adverts; a paid subscription removes them.
fact: Downloading songs from sites without permission breaks copyright law in most countries, pays the musicians nothing, and the sites can hide viruses and scams.
fact: Very loud music in headphones can damage hearing for good; a common tip is to keep the volume at about 60 percent and take breaks.
fact: An "E" next to a song means explicit, usually swearing or adult themes; many songs have a clean version.
fact: Okean Elzy is a Ukrainian rock band formed in 1994 and popular for decades.
fact: Many pop songs share the same four chords in the same order; songs differ in melody, words and sound, and using the same chords is allowed.
fact: Short video apps often use one catchy clip of a song; the full song usually has verses, a chorus and other parts.
fact: Liking or not liking a song is an opinion, not a fact.
exercise: Sofia has hummed one chorus for three days. What is it? | An earworm, which is common and harmless | A sign something is wrong with her brain | A hearing problem that needs a doctor | Proof the song is the best ever written
exercise: What might help a little with an earworm, according to one study? | Chewing gum | Holding your breath | Covering one ear | Singing it backwards
exercise: Ben thinks every play pays the singer a pound. What is closer to true? | A play is reported to pay a fraction of a penny, shared by many | Every single play pays the singer exactly one whole pound | Streaming pays nothing to anybody ever | The singer gets paid only when the song ends
exercise: How does Zoe's app make a playlist of "exactly her songs"? | An algorithm guesses from what she and others played | The app reads her mind through the screen | Somebody at the app listens to her all day | The singer picks the songs for each listener by hand every morning
exercise: Why does Wang Fang's free app keep playing adverts? | The adverts pay for the music and the app | The app is broken and needs fixing | Adverts are the law for every song | Music costs nothing, so adverts are a joke
exercise: Cian's cousin gets songs "free off some dodgy site". What is true? | It breaks copyright, pays the artist nothing and may hide viruses | It is fine because everything you find online is free to take and keep | It is legal if you only download a few | The singer gets paid more that way
exercise: Tunde's mate can hear his headphones. What does that suggest? | It's probably loud enough to harm his hearing | Headphones can't hurt young ears | It just means the headphones are top quality and expensive | It is fine as long as he likes the song
exercise: What is a common tip for safe headphone volume? | About 60 percent, with breaks | Full volume, but only for an hour | As loud as you like if you're under 12 | Turn it up until the street noise is gone
exercise: Amara sees an "E" next to a song. What does it mean? | Explicit, usually swearing or adult themes | Excellent, the song got top marks from the critics | Easy, a song good for beginners | English, the song is in English
exercise: Marko calls Dad's Okean Elzy song cringe. What is fairest? | Every generation has its music, and taste is not a ranking | Old music is always worse than new music, so Marko is right | New music is always worse than old music | Dad is wrong to like any music at all
exercise: Isla says four songs "sound the same, they copied". What is true? | Many hits share chords, and differ in tune, words and sound | Songs that use the same chords are illegal copies of each other | Only one song in the world may use those chords | Chords belong to the first band that used them
exercise: Nadia only knew 15 seconds of a song. Why? | Short video apps often use just one catchy clip | Every song is only 15 seconds long | The radio added extra parts to the song | TikTok songs are different songs from radio ones
exercise: Mason says a song is "bussin'"; his sister says "mid". Who is right? | Neither is wrong, liking a song is an opinion | Mason, because popular songs must be good | His sister, because popular songs must be bad | Whoever shouts loudest wins the argument
exercise: What is a royalty? | Money paid to music makers when their work is used | The crown worn by a pop king | A rule that every song must be short enough for the radio | A free song given to fans
exercise: When was Okean Elzy formed? | 1994 | 1954 | 2014 | 1874
test: Your mate goes, "if it's on the internet it's free to take, innit." Is he right? | No, songs belong to the people who made them | Yes, the internet makes everything free | Yes, but only on weekends | No, but only for songs in English
test: What would happen if everyone downloaded songs only from pirate sites? | Musicians would get paid nothing for their songs | Musicians would get much richer, because more people would listen | The songs would sound better | Nothing would change for anyone
test: Your nan reckons a song stuck in your head means "something's not right up there". What is the truth? | Earworms are very common and harmless | She is right, it is a kind of brain illness | Only children get earworms | Earworms last forever
test: A cartoon shows a phone app with a crystal ball reading a boy's mind to pick songs. What really happens? | The app guesses from listening history | Apps can really read minds | A singer phones each listener | The phone hears the boy's thoughts through the screen
test: Mum says "nothing's ever really free, love." How does that fit a free music app? | Someone pays, through adverts or subscriptions | It doesn't, free apps cost nobody anything | The government pays for every song | Musicians work for nothing on purpose
test: Mason's sister says the song is "mid" (average). What is she sharing? | Her opinion of the song | A fact about the song's chart position | The middle part of the song | That the song is half finished
test: Cian says the dodgy site is "deadly" (brilliant in Irish slang). What should he know? | It breaks the law and can hide viruses and scams | It is safe because his cousin uses it every day and nothing went wrong | Deadly sites are approved by the singers | Irish sites are allowed to share songs
test: Your cousin says a song with the same four chords as another is "a total rip-off". What is the best reply? | Chords are shared by everyone, the tune and words make it different | She's right, those songs should all be banned for copying each other | Only one song can ever use four chords | Chords are owned by the first singer forever
test: Little brother asks to listen to an "E" song. What is a sensible move? | Check the label and pick the clean version if unsure | Play it louder since E means excellent | Ignore the label, it means nothing | Delete the app altogether
test: Ben wants to support his favourite singer. What reportedly helps more than streaming? | Going to shows or buying from them directly | Streaming the song once more | Telling the singer online to try harder with the next song | Downloading it from a pirate site
test: What would happen to your ears after years of blasting music at full volume? | You could lose some hearing for good | Your ears would get used to it and toughen up | Nothing, ears always heal themselves | You would hear better than everyone else
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the remuneration of musicians by streaming services? | A sum reported to be a fraction of a penny per play, apportioned among several parties | A fixed sum of one pound per play, paid in full to the performer alone, without deductions | No remuneration whatsoever to any party | A single payment made upon the first play only
exam: Identify the principal basis upon which a music application compiles a personalised playlist. | The listening history of the user compared with that of other listeners | The direct apprehension of the user's thoughts | The personal selection of songs by each performer | A random selection without regard to the user
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the obtaining of recordings from unauthorised websites. | It contravenes copyright law in most jurisdictions and remunerates no creator | It is lawful provided the quantity obtained is small | It increases the income of the performers concerned | It is permitted in respect of all recordings in English
exam: Determine the most accurate characterisation of the phenomenon known as an earworm. | A common and harmless involuntary recurrence of a melody in the mind | A disorder of the brain requiring medical treatment | A permanent impairment of the hearing | An indication of the superior quality of the composition
